],"likely_direction":"mixed","interpretation_effect":"The assessment retained both the professional monetary framework and fiscal and investment weaknesses."}],"confidence_and_range_effect":"High-quality IMF evidence supports macro conclusions, but partial-year tenure and long-standing structural problems reduce attribution confidence.","remaining_uncertainty":"Prayut left office in August, so full-year outcomes and successor policies overlap.","report_volume_not_used_as_severity":true,"no_blanket_regime_correction":true},"chapter_rationale":"Prayut Chanocha’s 2023 economic stewardship was mixed but maintained stability, supporting 5.5. Thailand’s inflation fell, the central bank retained professional operational autonomy, and tourism and consumption supported recovery. At the same time, growth was modest, investment weak, household debt elevated, and fiscal space constrained. Long-standing gaps in skills, regional opportunity, women’s employment, agricultural productivity, and education limited the breadth of prosperity. Competition-law exemptions for state enterprises and public-interest activities also weakened confidence in uniformly fair rules, although some exemptions may be legitimate. Prayut governed only through August, and tourism, global demand, and inherited pandemic conditions shaped the full-year result. Stability and capable institutions place the record above lower cases, but limited structural correction and persistent inequality prevent a 6.5–7 judgment.","lower_anchor_rejected":"Falling inflation, professional monetary institutions, recovery, and preserved financial stability exceed a below-middle record.","higher_anchor_rejected":"Weak investment, debt risks, inequality, and limited structural reform prevent a substantially stronger appraisal."}
{"dossier_job_key":"dossier:2023-luna-next4-v2:2023:MEX:15802","iso3":"MEX","ruler_name":"Andrés Manuel López Obrador","score_1_to_10":5.0,"insufficient_evidence_reason":null,"decisive_positive_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E026","explanation":"Large welfare and pension increases were concrete distributional choices."},{"evidence_id":"E029","explanation":"Independent central-bank data document continued growth."},{"evidence_id":"E022","explanation":"Independent monetary analysis remained available while macroeconomic continuity was preserved."}],"decisive_negative_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E023","explanation":"Large cost escalation, delay, and incomplete opening materially weaken productive-investment performance."},{"evidence_id":"E025","explanation":"Uneven health and disability funding qualifies the fairness of budget priorities."}],"inherited_baseline_and_constraints":"AMLO inherited poverty, informality, regional inequality, weak services, and oil dependence while benefiting from central-bank continuity and external demand.","ruler_attribution":"AMLO strongly owned flagship investment and welfare priorities; the independent central bank, external demand, and private activity shared macro outcomes.","contrary_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E023","explanation":"The Maya Train may eventually produce tourism and regional benefits, but these were not established in 2023."}],"source_mix":"Central-bank and fiscal records, budget analysis, project reporting, World Bank and CONEVAL context, and peer-reviewed wage research.","structured_prior_summary":"The available local package supplies longitudinal country-level growth, poverty, labor, investment, distribution, and trajectory evidence. It is not independent ruler attribution.","bias_assessment":{"material_biases":[{"bias":"Flagship-project reporting emphasizes cost escalation, while government claims emphasize future regional benefits.","supporting_evidence_ids":["E023"],"likely_direction":"mixed","interpretation_effect":"The Maya Train was judged on documented cost and incomplete delivery; potential benefits were retained as uncertain contrary evidence."},{"bias":"Budget allocations do not establish service delivery or transfer adequacy.","supporting_evidence_ids":["E025","E026"],"likely_direction":"favors_ruler","interpretation_effect":"Welfare expansion was credited as priority-setting but not equated with broad successful outcomes."}],"confidence_and_range_effect":"Independent fiscal and central-bank evidence supports moderate-high confidence, but project benefits and health-budget classification remain uncertain.","remaining_uncertainty":"Final Maya Train costs and benefits, health-system effects, and distributional outcomes remain unresolved.","report_volume_not_used_as_severity":true,"no_blanket_regime_correction":true},"chapter_rationale":"Andrés Manuel López Obrador’s 2023 record was mixed, supporting 5.0. Mexico grew 3.2 percent, maintained an independent central bank, expanded welfare spending and old-age pensions, and continued a minimum-wage strategy associated with higher labor income. Those broadly oriented policies coexist with significant weaknesses. The Maya Train opened only partially after major delays and very large cost escalation. Debt service rose, oil revenue fell, and a limited share of new financing went to physical investment. Health and disability funding was uneven even as pensions expanded, while poverty, informality, regional disparity, and service deprivation remained substantial. External demand and post-pandemic recovery contributed to growth, so it is not credited solely to AMLO. Macroeconomic continuity and social redistribution prevent a lower score; inefficient flagship investment and uneven public-service priorities prevent a 6–7 appraisal.","lower_anchor_rejected":"Growth, monetary credibility, expanded transfers, and wage policy prevent a predominantly harmful assessment.","higher_anchor_rejected":"Flagship-project inefficiency, fiscal pressure, and uneven services are too material for a 6–7 score."}
