on spending was not treated as electoral largesse, but was separated from pre-election expansion."},{"bias":"Institutional reports document system-level failures more reliably than personal motives.","supporting_evidence_ids":["E030","E033","E034"],"likely_direction":"uncertain","interpretation_effect":"Procurement weakness and central-bank pressure were attributed through Erdoğan’s formal authority without alleging unproven personal enrichment."}],"confidence_and_range_effect":"Strong institutional evidence supports high confidence, while earthquake costs and the midyear policy reversal broaden the plausible range.","remaining_uncertainty":"The durability of post-election normalization and allocation of earthquake spending remained unresolved.","report_volume_not_used_as_severity":true,"no_blanket_regime_correction":true},"chapter_rationale":"Recep Tayyip Erdoğan’s 2023 economic stewardship was poor, supporting 3.0. Before the May election, monetary policy kept deeply negative real interest rates despite very high inflation, under a presidency with extensive power over central-bank leadership. Fiscal policy was also strongly expansionary, combining necessary earthquake response with pre-election largesse. After the election, new personnel tightened monetary policy and simplified distortive measures, a meaningful correction that prevents an even lower score. Nevertheless, market institutions, parliamentary budget scrutiny, procurement transparency, and competition were weak, while external deficits and foreign-currency exposure remained serious. The earthquake was an extraordinary external shock and reconstruction spending is not treated as misconduct. Even after that adjustment, repeated destabilizing choices and weak accountability dominated the year; the late reversal had not restored stability or household security by year-end.","lower_anchor_rejected":"Professional appointments and a genuine post-election stabilization pivot prevent a score of 1–2.","higher_anc